---
/**
 * Where the figures come from, how they are checked, and what is deliberately not here.
 *
 * This site's whole posture is that a reader can check it. That argument previously lived only in
 * the repository's README files, which is to say it was available to contributors and to nobody
 * else. It is a page now.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { loadCorpus } from "../lib/corpus.ts";
import { loadFeed } from "../lib/feed.ts";
import { count, millions, pct } from "../lib/format.ts";
import { DENOMINATORS, denominatorSource, denominatorSpan } from "../lib/denominators.ts";
import * as routes from "../lib/routes.ts";
import { yearChip, yearOf } from "../lib/year.ts";
import { scatterSpec } from "../lib/plot/spec.ts";
import { renderToString } from "../lib/plot/ssr.ts";
import { pairs } from "../lib/relationships.ts";
import SectionAnchor from "../components/SectionAnchor.astro";

const { bundle, verification, forecastable } = loadFeed();
const corpus = loadCorpus();
const s = bundle.statewide;
const meta = bundle.projection;
// The MR-81 endpoints, read rather than typed — the pair that went stale twice. See
// `denominatorSpan`.
const span = denominatorSpan(bundle, "meal_program");

/*
 * The reduction-factor model against the rates county auditors actually charged.
 *
 * The same quantity twice — mills — so the two axes share a domain and the identity line is a
 * real 45°. A district on it is one `crates/millage` reproduces; the vertical distance off it is
 * the residual the district's own tax page states in the same units.
 *
 * The counts are read off the feed rather than written down. "182 of 273" is the kind of sentence
 * that is true when it is written and wrong two bundles later with nothing to notice.
 */
const withMillage = bundle.districts.filter((d) => d.millage != null);
const EXACT = 0.01; // The precision Table SD-1 publishes to.
const atFloor = withMillage.filter((d) => d.millage!.at_floor);
const aboveFloor = withMillage.filter((d) => !d.millage!.at_floor);
const isExact = (d: (typeof withMillage)[number]) => Math.abs(d.millage!.residual) < EXACT;
const millage = {
  total: withMillage.length,
  exact: withMillage.filter(isExact).length,
  atFloor: atFloor.length,
  floorExact: atFloor.filter(isExact).length,
  aboveFloor: aboveFloor.length,
  aboveExact: aboveFloor.filter(isExact).length,
  over: withMillage.filter((d) => d.millage!.residual > 0.5).length,
  under: withMillage.filter((d) => d.millage!.residual < -0.5).length,
  /* How many districts the busiest single coordinate holds. Counted rather than asserted, because
     it is the number that says how much the picture is hiding. */
  stacked: (() => {
    const at: Record<string, number> = {};
    for (const d of withMillage) {
      const k = `${d.millage!.predicted_rate.toFixed(2)},${d.millage!.observed_rate.toFixed(2)}`;
      at[k] = (at[k] ?? 0) + 1;
    }
    return Math.max(...Object.values(at));
  })(),
};
const millageScatter = renderToString(
  scatterSpec(
    pairs(
      withMillage,
      (d) => d.millage!.predicted_rate,
      (d) => d.millage!.observed_rate,
      (d, predicted, observed) =>
        `${d.name}: the factors predict ${predicted.toFixed(2)} mills, Table SD-1 charges ${observed.toFixed(2)}` +
        (d.millage!.at_floor ? ", at the twenty-mill floor" : ""),
      /*
       * Split by floor status, which is the variable that explains the fit rather than a proxy for
       * it. Banding by valuation instead was tried and is mostly this in disguise: the terciles'
       * exact-reproduction counts run 80 / 68 / 38 and their at-floor counts 109 / 99 / 62, so the
       * ramp would spend three ordinal steps re-drawing a two-state fact. Two states take the
       * categorical pair.
       */
      { series: (d) => (d.millage!.at_floor ? "guarantee" : "formula") },
    ),
    {
      x: { label: "what the reduction factors predict, in mills", format: (v) => v.toFixed(0) },
      y: { label: "what the county auditor charges", format: (v) => v.toFixed(0) },
    },
    [],
    { identity: { label: "predicted = charged" } },
  ),
);
---

<Base
  title="Method"
  description={`How the figures on this site are produced: the department's own FY${bundle.fiscal_year} model, re-run and checked against ${verification.comparisons.length} reference scenarios before the site is allowed to build.`}
  image={routes.og.page("method")}
  imageAlt="How these figures are made, and the checkpoints they must reproduce before the site builds"
  section="method"
>
  <h1>Method</h1>
  <p class="sub">
    Where every figure comes from, what checks it passed to get here, and what this site
    deliberately does not show.
  </p>

  <div class="card" id="computed-twice" data-part="computed-twice">
    <h2><SectionAnchor id="computed-twice" />The formula is computed twice, on purpose</h2>
    <p class="note">
      Ohio's Fair School Funding Plan is implemented in this repository twice: once in Rust, in{" "}
      <code>crates/foundation</code> and <code>crates/project</code>, and once in TypeScript, in{" "}
      <code>src/lib/policy.ts</code>. Two implementations of one formula is normally a bad trade —
      they drift, and the one nobody runs is the one that is wrong.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      What makes it acceptable is that <strong>the Rust is authoritative and the TypeScript has to
      prove it agrees</strong>. The feed carries {verification.comparisons.length} scenarios and {
        verification.forecasts.length
      } forecasts computed by the Rust, and the TypeScript must reproduce every one of them —
      against the real {count(s.districts)}-district panel, to within a dollar across seven
      billion — before this site is allowed to build. It reproduced all of them for this build.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      That is not a formality. Wiring the check up found a bug <em>in the Rust</em>: a district
      pulled below a raised minimum state share was taking the branch meant for districts already
      on the floor, and having its published share scaled by the ratio of the minimums instead of
      by the floor itself. It was worth $4.7 million, and 273 Rust tests did not catch it, because
      the wrong answer is still an increase and still confined to districts below the new floor.
      The second implementation disagreed, and the disagreement was the signal.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      The check runs in two places for two different reasons. At <strong>build</strong> it is a
      gate: it fails the build, so a drifted formula is never published. On <strong
        ><a href="/scenario">the scenario routes</a></strong
      > it runs again in the browser, against the panel actually served — because that file can be
      replaced without a rebuild, and a check that only ran where the answer was already known
      would be decorative.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="card" id="pupil-counts" data-part="pupil-counts">
    <h2><SectionAnchor id="pupil-counts" />Six pupil counts, and which one each figure uses</h2>
    <p class="note">
      Ohio's sources do not agree on how many children a district has. Not because any of them is
      wrong — they count different things, for different purposes, in different years. The
      Department of Taxation counts children who live in the district; the Department of Education
      counts the ones it teaches, and separately the ones it funds, and separately again a
      need-weighted count. For Columbus those range from 43,019 to 73,746.
    </p>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table class="prose">
        <tbody>
          {
            Object.values(DENOMINATORS).map((d) => (
              <tr>
                <th>{d.label}</th>
                <td>
                  <div class="n">{denominatorSource(d)}</div>
                  {d.note}
                  {"endpoints" in d && span && (
                    <>
                      {" "}Sponsors rise from {count(span.first)} in FY{span.firstYear} to{" "}
                      {count(span.last)} in FY{span.lastYear}.
                    </>
                  )}
                </td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      Two figures divided by different counts cannot be compared, and nothing about them looks
      wrong when they are: both are numbers, both are per pupil, and both are correct. This site
      has published that error twice — most recently a district's tax-table valuation per pupil
      set against a statewide median computed on the education department's count, which was
      right for most of the state and wrong by a factor of two for the districts a reader is most
      likely to open. <code>src/lib/denominators.ts</code> now declares the denominator of every
      per-pupil field in the feed, and a test fails the build if a new one arrives undeclared or
      if a pair the site renders side by side stops matching.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="card" id="model-and-record" data-part="model-and-record">
    <h2><SectionAnchor id="model-and-record" />What is a model, and what is a record</h2>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table class="prose">
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th>The funding figures</th>
            <td>
              A <strong>model</strong>, and the department's rather than this repository's. The
              per-district figures are read from its FY{bundle.fiscal_year} TRAD State Foundation
              Funding Calculator, not recomputed — with one exception, below. What gets recomputed
              here is what a <em>different</em> decision would produce. It is a projection of what
              districts will be paid, not a record of what they were.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>The five-year forecast figures</th>
            <td>
              A <strong>record</strong>. Audited actuals from each district's own filing — money
              that changed hands. These are the only figures on this site that are not computed,
              which is why they have <a href="/districts">their own route per district</a> and are
              never placed as a check on the model. The two are differently constructed and are not
              comparable line for line.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>The outcome figures</th>
            <td>
              A <strong>measurement</strong>, of pupils and not of districts. The {yearOf("outcome.performance")} Ohio
              School Report Card. Every association drawn from it is a correlation over {
                s.outcomes ? count(s.outcomes.districts) : "—"
              } districts and none of them identifies an effect. <a href="/outcomes">Why that
              matters here in particular</a>.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>The property tax figures</th>
            <td>
              A <strong>record</strong>, and from a different department. Table SD-1 is published
              by the Ohio Department of Taxation rather than the Department of Education, which
              makes it the one place two arms of the state describe the same district
              independently — and where they overlap they agree, to 0.01 mills across all 606
              districts carrying both. Two tax years, because H.B. 920 is a mechanism that only
              exists as a change.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>The spending-by-function figures</th>
            <td>
              A <strong>record</strong>, from the report card, per pupil, on unweighted ADM. Not
              the audited actuals beside them on the same page: different source, different basis,
              and not summable with them.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>The H.B. 920 figures</th>
            <td>
              <strong>Computed</strong> — the one block on the property tax page that is. The
              predicted rate, the residual against it, and what one mill raises come from{" "}
              <code>crates/millage</code> applied to the two tax years, not from any published
              column. Running it against the tables found a defect in each direction: the crate
              reduced rates below the twenty-mill floor for the six districts that never voted
              twenty mills, and this site reported those districts as being <em>above</em> the
              floor with reduction factors operative. Both are fixed, and{" "}
              <code>crates/millage/tests/against_published_tables.rs</code> now holds the
              calculator to 1,218 published district-years.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>The local capacity measure</th>
            <td>
              <strong>Computed, and the one figure on this site verified exactly.</strong> R.C.
              3317.017 blends 60% of a district's assessed valuation with two income measures and
              scales the result by a rate that rises with income and stops at 2.5%.{" "}
              <code>crates/local-capacity</code> runs that statute and reproduces the department's
              own published answer for <strong>all 609 districts, on both the capacity and the
              rate, to within a hundredth of a percent</strong>.
              <div class="n">
                It was got wrong first. Reconstructing four of the inputs rather than reading them
                left the calculation 4.4% light — a near-uniform error that correlated with none
                of the three terms, which is what a shared wrong input looks like rather than a
                wrong formula. The department publishes all four; the residual is now zero.
              </div>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>The charge-off comparison</th>
            <td>
              A <strong>counterfactual</strong>, and the only thing on this site that is. It holds
              the plan's own base cost fixed and substitutes the mechanism it replaced — a flat 23
              statutory mills against valuation — for the local capacity measure. It is not a
              reconstruction of any year the charge-off governed, and it reaches one row of the
              calculation, because that is the only pair of components the corpus declares
              comparable. Every figure is on the pre-2008 total-taxable base and so overstates
              what the charge-off would have taken.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>The federal spending share</th>
            <td>
              A <strong>record</strong>, from the report card, and the only figure here that
              measures exposure to a decision Ohio does not make. Reported as a share rather than
              as dollars because both its parts divide by a need-weighted pupil count and their
              ratio does not — the one spending statistic on this site that can be set beside
              another district's without asking which denominator either uses.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>Growth</th>
            <td>
              Two published measures, not one: value-added over three years and over one. This
              site uses the three-year figure everywhere, which is the department's own default
              and the right one — but it is a choice, and the district pages now show both. They
              never substantively disagree; of the 534 districts printing non-zero on both, the 44
              that point opposite ways are all within 0.04 of zero.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>The interstate comparison</th>
            <td>
              A <strong>measurement</strong>, and the only federal source here. The Census
              Bureau's Annual Survey of School System Finances counts every school system in the
              country on one set of definitions, which is what lets this site say whether Ohio is
              unusual rather than only what Ohio does. FY2022, the peak year of pandemic relief —
              which deflates the state and local shares and so understates the finding rather
              than inflating it.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>Valuation per pupil</th>
            <td>
              Two published figures, not one. Taxation and Education agree on the valuation to the
              dollar and divide it by different pupil counts — 43,019 against 73,746 for Columbus.
              A third count, base cost ADM, is what base cost per pupil divides by. Each page
              names which it is using; the property tax route uses Taxation's and everything else
              uses Education's, because that is the one the formula reads.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>The projection</th>
            <td>
              A <strong>forecast</strong>, and the only thing on this site that is. Enrollment is
              carried forward by a damped trend and the formula is re-run at it; nothing projects
              the formula, the appropriation, or assessed valuation. Its interval is drawn as the
              headline rather than as a disclaimer, for reasons below.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card" id="base-cost" data-part="base-cost">
    <h2><SectionAnchor id="base-cost" />The one thing here that is computed</h2>
    <p class="note">
      Base cost is the exception to the row above. It is not a rate the state sets: R.C. 3317.011
      assembles it for each district from statutory staffing ratios applied to that district's own
      grade-band enrollment, priced at statewide average salaries — twenty-two elements in five
      sub-components. <code>crates/foundation</code> runs that statute, and every district page
      shows the result rather than the total.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      Which is a claim, so it is reconciled rather than asserted. The department publishes its own
      aggregate for every district; the build-up is shown beside it with the difference stated. The
      worst of the {count(s.districts)} is <strong>$1.09</strong> on figures in the millions, and
      the residuals cancel to nothing statewide — twenty-two elements each rounded at the point the
      department rounds them. The reproduction is checked across the whole panel in{" "}
      <code>crates/foundation</code>'s tests before this site is allowed to build.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      This is the lever the <a href="/wiki/scenario/fsfp-input-year-refresh">cost input refresh</a>{" "}
      question turns on: the ratios and the ADM definitions are fixed in statute, and the prices are
      a reference year the General Assembly chooses. H.B. 110 priced FY2022 from FY2018 salaries,
      H.B. 33 refreshed the inputs to FY2022, and H.B. 96 held them there through FY{
        bundle.fiscal_year
      }.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="card" id="reduction-factors" data-part="reduction-factors">
    <h2><SectionAnchor id="reduction-factors" />And the second, checked the same way<Fragment set:html={yearChip("millage")} /></h2>
    <p class="note">
      The card above says base cost is the exception on the <em>funding</em> side. There is one
      more on the property-tax side: <code>crates/millage</code> runs H.B. 920's reduction factors
      forward from each district's prior charged rate, applies the statutory floor, and predicts
      what it should be charging now. Table SD-1 publishes what it actually charges. The two are
      the same quantity arrived at two ways, so they can be drawn against each other and the
      distance between them read off the axis.
    </p>
    <div class="chartwrap" data-chart="reduction-factors" set:html={millageScatter} />
    <div class="legend">
      <span><i class="sw formula"></i> Above the floor — the factors set the rate</span>
      <span><i class="sw guarantee"></i> At the twenty-mill floor — they have stopped</span>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      {/* `{" "}` before every expression that opens a line: Astro trims the newline between text
          and an adjacent expression exactly as JSX does, and "for188 of 609" is what that looks
          like. The e2e suite scans every route for the tag-boundary form of this and cannot see
          this one, because it leaves no tag behind. */}
      A district on the line is one the factors account for exactly. <strong>They do, for{" "}
      {millage.exact} of {millage.total}</strong> — and where they do is the part worth stating:{" "}
      {millage.floorExact} of the {millage.atFloor} districts at the twenty-mill floor land on the
      line, against {millage.aboveExact} of the {millage.aboveFloor} above it. At the floor the
      reduction factors have stopped operating and there is nothing left to predict; above it they
      are what sets the rate, and that is where the model is approximate.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      Which the picture understates, because agreement has one coordinate and disagreement has
      many. <strong>{millage.stacked} of the districts at the floor are the single dot at
      (20.00, 20.00)</strong>, and the visible orange spike above it is the {millage.atFloor -
      millage.floorExact} that the floor does not account for. Read the counts here rather than the
      density: a scatter draws the exceptions and hides the rule whenever the rule is one value.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      The departures are almost all in one direction.{" "}
      {millage.over} districts charge more than the
      factors predict by over half a mill and {millage.under} charge less — which is the asymmetry
      the factors themselves imply, since they reduce existing levies on existing property and
      know nothing of a levy passed since or a building put up since. Each district's own page
      says which of the two its residual is consistent with; this repository does not hold the levy
      histories that would settle it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="card" id="forecast-range" data-part="forecast-range">
    <h2><SectionAnchor id="forecast-range" />Why the forecast leads with a range</h2>
    <p class="note">
      A forecast rendered as "$7.13B (±3.4%)" is read as $7.13B. The interval becomes a disclaimer
      the eye skips, which is worse than showing no interval at all, because it lends a forecast
      the authority of a measurement. So the tile's large number is the <strong>range</strong> and
      the central estimate is the small line underneath, labelled as one path through the band. In
      the chart the band is a mark rather than shading, its centre line is dashed, only the two
      bounds are direct-labelled, and the y axis — truncated, because a band a few percent wide is
      invisible against a zero baseline — says so on its face.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      The band is <strong>not</strong> any district's own historical variability; three
      observations cannot give that. It is the {meta ? meta.prior_source : "cross-sectional spread"}
      {meta ? `, σ = ${pct(meta.sigma, 2)} a year` : ""}, widened by the square root of the horizon
      because growth errors compound. Both ends are computed by re-running the whole formula at
      that enrollment rather than by scaling the middle: the guarantee is a <code>max</code>, and a
      district can be on formula at one end of the band and on the guarantee at the other.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      The forecast card is always below the simulation and is never merged with it, and there is
      deliberately no figure anywhere on this site that adds them together. Re-running the formula
      with a lever moved is exact; projecting enrollment six years out is not, and a combined
      number would inherit the second's error while wearing the first's precision.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="card" id="provenance" data-part="provenance">
    <h2><SectionAnchor id="provenance" />Provenance</h2>
    <p class="note">{bundle.provenance}</p>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table class="prose">
        <tbody>
          <tr><th>Bundle contract</th><td>{bundle.contract_version}</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Districts in the model</th><td>{count(s.districts)}</td></tr>
          <tr>
            <th>Reference scenarios reproduced</th>
            <td>{verification.comparisons.length} of {verification.comparisons.length}</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>Reference forecasts reproduced</th>
            <td>
              {forecastable
                ? `${verification.forecasts.length} of ${verification.forecasts.length}`
                : "check did not pass — no band is drawn on this build"}
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr><th>Price index</th><td>{bundle.deflator?.label ?? "none"}</td></tr>
          <tr>
            <th>Corpus</th>
            <td>
              {corpus.nodes.length} nodes, {corpus.classes.length} classes,{" "}
              {corpus.sources.length} <a href="/wiki/source">sources</a>
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      Every source the corpus draws on is listed at <a href="/wiki/source">/wiki/source</a>, and
      the feed itself is downloadable at <a href="/data">/data</a>. Thirteen of the retrieved files
      are pinned by SHA-256 in the repository, so a claim can be traced to a byte-identical copy of
      the publication it came from.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="card" id="not-here" data-part="not-here">
    <h2><SectionAnchor id="not-here" />What is not here</h2>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table class="prose">
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th>Year-over-year funding</th>
            <td>
              The department publishes one foundation calculator at a time and replaces rather than
              archives it, so no FY{bundle.fiscal_year - 1} per-district payment figure exists in
              this repository to compare against. What the district pages show instead is the FY{
                bundle.fiscal_year
              } formula held fixed and run at each of three enrollment years, which isolates the
              enrollment channel exactly — something two years of published totals could not do,
              because the formula moved between them too.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>The regime history</th>
            <td>
              Walking the numbers from the Foundation Program through to the Fair School Funding
              Plan needs a historical series the corpus does not yet have. The regimes themselves
              are documented — <a href="/wiki/funding-regime">five of them</a> — but without
              per-district figures attached.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>Property values under a scenario</th>
            <td>
              Assessed valuation is held fixed in every scenario. Local capacity is 60% valuation,
              so a proposal that moves property values is out of reach: the corpus has one
              observation per district and cannot project it.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>Tax received, as opposed to charged</th>
            <td>
              Table SD-1 reports what is <em>charged</em> for a tax year. Collection happens across
              the following calendar year in two settlements, so a levy passed in November is
              charged in full and arrives across two fiscal years. Every ratio here that puts a
              charge over a year's spending inherits that gap, and it is largest exactly where a
              levy has just passed — which is one of the three districts charged more than it
              spends.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>Community schools and scholarships</th>
            <td>
              The deduction that routes money from a resident district to a community school or a
              scholarship is not modelled here. The {count(s.districts)} districts in this feed are
              the traditional districts in the state's own calculator.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card" id="checking-it-yourself" data-part="checking-it-yourself">
    <h2><SectionAnchor id="checking-it-yourself" />Checking it yourself</h2>
    <p class="note">
      Everything here is reproducible from the repository. The feed is regenerated with one
      command, the site is built with a second, and continuous integration diffs the committed feed
      against a fresh one so the two cannot silently disagree.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      Total state foundation aid in this model is{" "}
      {millions(s.realized_aid_total).replace("+", "")} across {count(s.districts)} districts, of which {millions(s.guarantee_total).replace("+", "")}
      {" "}is paid by the guarantee to {s.on_guarantee} districts. If a figure on this site
      disagrees with the department's own, that is a defect and the repository is where to file it.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
